  "content": {
    "id": "v2-6283f-p10-practice-slot-6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "If $\\sec\\theta=\\dfrac{k}{2}$, where $k>2$, find the possible values of $\\sin\\theta$ and $\\cot\\theta$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\sin\\theta=\\pm\\dfrac{\\sqrt{k^2-4}}{k}$, $\\cot\\theta=\\pm\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{k^2-4}}$, with matching signs.",
      "worked": "Since $\\sec\\theta>0$, $\\cos\\theta>0$, so $\\theta$ is in quadrant I or IV. In a reference right-angled triangle, take adjacent side $2$ and hypotenuse $k$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\mathrm{Opp}&=\\sqrt{k^2-2^2}\\\\&=\\sqrt{k^2-4}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nIn quadrant I, both sine and cotangent are positive:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin\\theta&=\\frac{\\sqrt{k^2-4}}{k}\\\\\\cot\\theta&=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{k^2-4}}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nIn quadrant IV, both are negative:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin\\theta&=-\\frac{\\sqrt{k^2-4}}{k}\\\\\\cot\\theta&=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{k^2-4}}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": null
  },
